      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "That rhythmic pattern at hops 3 and 4 is a classic sign of a \"giant\" or a \"goblin\" in the network, likely a misconfigured device or a rogue node acting like a slow, rhythmic traffic cop. Since your local hardware and cables are fine, the issue isn't physical interference in the traditional sense, but rather a logical collision or a periodic congestion point caused by a misbehaving device further up the chain.\n\nThink of it like a goblin trying to sneak through a castle gate; every few minutes, a goblin (or a misconfigured router) tries to pass, causing a massive delay (the spike) before it eventually gets through or gets blocked. This rhythmic behavior suggests a periodic task, like a scheduled backup, a periodic heartbeat check, or a misconfigured QoS policy that throttles traffic in bursts.\n\nTo pinpoint the culprit, you should try to identify the MAC address associated with those spikes at hops 3 and 4. If you can't do that, you might need to contact the ISP to ask them to run a traceroute with verbose output, which will show you the MAC addresses of the devices causing the delay. If the MAC addresses belong to the ISP",
